{"author":{"id":"6af04b5756f10b3005a23338b8f25a6c1d7564fe1ddddc77e70806b2f14ac31a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789498182,"nonce":"dhAIgjvwNDKtHs+vVzuAqshBdjA=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOUKdEis8AAAABRc5bCw","github-url":"https://github.com/ff6347/gic-lang/issues/36","origin":"github"},"title":"Spike the Tauri application shell","message":"## Parent\n\ngit-bug issue `60b2077` — PRD: GIC v0.9 workshop environment\n\n## What to build\n\nBuild disposable evidence for hosting the shared static GIC web application in a dedicated Tauri 2 window, prioritizing a small native shell over Electron. Determine whether packaged macOS and Windows applications can load built assets, use native Open/Save/Save As dialogs, handle lifecycle and callback events, and expose only operation-specific privileged commands. The static authoring shell must not require a Node sidecar; assess a sidecar separately as a possible tutor/provider boundary.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A minimal packaged application launches built assets without a development server or companion process.\n- [ ] Native Open, Save, and Save As operations round-trip one temporary `.gic` file through a restricted adapter.\n- [ ] Tauri capabilities and commands do not expose unrestricted filesystem, shell, or credential access to the webview.\n- [ ] Close lifecycle behavior and one application callback path are demonstrated or their blocking limitations are recorded.\n- [ ] Tauri, Rust, webview, package, operating-system, and architecture versions used by the evidence are recorded.\n- [ ] macOS packaging is exercised locally; exact Windows packaging and smoke-test requirements are documented when a Windows runner is unavailable.\n- [ ] The result decides whether Tauri can host the static application without a sidecar and identifies any boundary that would require one for the optional tutor.\n- [ ] Disposable spike code is clearly separated from production implementation.\n\n## Product direction\n\nAvoid Electron unless evidence shows that Tauri cannot meet the required shell, file, lifecycle, or optional-provider boundaries without disproportionate complexity.\n\n## Blocked by\n\nNone — can start immediately.","files":null}]}